  "query_preview": "..An active 7 to 10 days is on the horizon across the\nBeehive State, with multiple rounds of precipitation expected.\n\nLate morning upper air and satellite analysis indicates an upper\nlevel low is shifting across the northern Intermountain Region.\nThe associated cold front continues to cross Utah, bringing a\nperiod of strong west to northwest winds. Any elevated winds will\ndecrease near 6 PM. Wind advisories remain in effect...and no\nchanges were made with this package.\n\nGiven the strength of this cold front, colder temperatures will\nshift into the region in its wake. Freezing Warnings remain in\neffect for many central and southern Utah valleys currently in \ntheir growing seasons for tonight into Thursday morning, though \nfreezing temperatures are not expected to the Wasatch Front or \nlower Washington County. Another cold night is expected Thursday\nnight into Friday morning and thus Freeze Watches are now in\neffect for all the same valleys as tonight with the addition of\neastern Box Elder County.\n\nAfter several days of gradual warming, a series of upper level\nlows will begin to impact Utah Saturday afternoon, continuing\nthrough at least early the next week. While there is a wide spread\nin timing, strength and even orientation of the various potential\nshortwave troughs and upper level lows, the main message is\nincreasing in confidence: temperatures at or below normal will\ncombine with periods of precipitation through the period for most\nof the state. The current deterministic NBM (National Blend of \nModels) indicates valleys north of Nephi will see 0.50-1.00\" of \nrain between 00Z Sunday and 00Z Tuesday, with valleys across \ncentral southern Utah seeing lower values closer to 0.10-0.50\". \nBased on the current deterministic snow levels and snow ratios, \nthe northern mountains would see around 6 to 12 inches of snow \nover this time period, with locally up to 18 inches in the upper \nCottonwoods. The central and southern mountains would be closer to\n4 to 8 inches. But those are deterministic values. If, for \nexample, we look at the 10th to 90th percentile for the upper \nCottonwoods, the variability in the ensembles become more obvious.\nThis range is currently 0.40-3.20 inches. The Salt Lake City \nAirport, as another examples, shows a significant variability \nbetween the 10th and 90th percentile: 0.10-1.40 inches. This \nperiod of active weather will be something to watch as the \nensemble members start to cluster more meaningfully into \nparticular solutions.",
